## Lift the graph-analytics triple cap via engine-side SQL aggregation

Context: KG Analytics was capped at `ONTOBRICKS_ANALYTICS_MAX_TRIPLES` (500,000)
and rejected larger graphs outright, because `GraphBuilder._load_triples`
materialises every triple into a Python list and `GraphMetrics` builds one
in-memory `networkx.Graph`. Phase A of the Lakeflow-pushdown plan removes that
wall without any new infrastructure: oversized graphs are analysed by SQL
aggregations executed inside the graph store, and entity-type filters are now
pushed down so a filtered analysis reads only the selected subgraph.

Removing the memory cap also exposed a second ceiling — `MetricsResult` carried
five floats plus a type and a label for *every* node, stored as one JSONB
`result` column and shipped whole to the browser. That payload is now bounded.

Branch: `feat/graph-analytics-lakeflow-pushdown` (from `develop`).

Changes:

1. src/back/core/graphdb/GraphDBBackend.py
   Add the analytics aggregation family as default-SQL methods, so Delta and
   Lakebase inherit them and only non-SQL engines need an override:
   `get_graph_structure_stats`, `get_top_nodes_by_degree`, `get_type_edge_stats`,
   `get_type_predicate_pairs`, plus the shared `_analytics_edge_cte` /
   `_degree_cte` fragments. The entity-entity edge definition is passed in by
   the caller (`excluded_predicates`) so storage stays free of analytics policy.
   Self-loops are excluded — a documented difference from NetworkX, which counts
   a self-loop as degree 2.
   Also add `query_triples_for_analysis`, which scopes a filtered load to the
   selected instances plus their direct neighbours, and its Python equivalent
   `_filter_analysis_triples_in_python` used by non-SQL engines and as the test
   oracle. `rdf:type` / `rdfs:label` are always retained: they never become
   edges but the analysis needs them for node types and display labels.
2. src/back/core/graph_analysis/PushdownMetrics.py
   New service assembling the same `MetricsResult` from those aggregations, with
   `supports_pushdown()` as the engine capability probe. Reports PageRank,
   clustering, betweenness, closeness and the component count through
   `unavailable_metrics` instead of returning zeros or approximations labelled
   like exact values.
3. src/back/core/graph_analysis/profiles.py
   Extract the temporal-predicate detection and flat-dataset heuristic out of
   `GraphMetrics` so both compute paths share one copy and cannot drift on what
   counts as flat.
4. src/back/core/graph_analysis/GraphMetrics.py
   Use the shared profile helpers; drop the now-duplicated private copies.
5. src/back/core/graph_analysis/GraphBuilder.py
   `_load_triples` pushes class/predicate filters down to the store when it
   supports it, so the cap applies to the reduced set. Reword the guard message,
   which previously told users filters could not help.
6. src/back/core/graph_analysis/models.py
   Add `mode` (`in_memory` | `pushdown`) and `unavailable_metrics` to
   `MetricsResult`; make `MetricsStats.connected_components` optional so an
   unknown count renders as em-dash rather than a misleading 0. Add `to_dict()`
   on each model, replacing the hand-rolled serialisation in `DigitalTwin` so
   both paths emit an identical payload. Document that `nodes` is bounded in
   pushdown mode and that node counts must come from `stats.node_count`.
7. src/back/objects/digitaltwin/DigitalTwin.py
   `compute_graph_metrics` gains `mode` / `top_n` / `allow_pushdown_fallback`:
   a class-filtered run over the cap is attempted in memory first and degrades
   to pushdown only if the subgraph still does not fit. `run_metrics_task`
   records the effective mode on the task result and in the registry row.
   Drops ~35 lines of duplicated serialisation.
8. src/api/routers/internal/dtwin.py
   `/metrics/compute` selects a mode instead of rejecting oversized graphs, and
   only errors when pushdown is genuinely unavailable (non-SQL engine or the
   setting disabled). `/dtwin/sync/stats` returns
   `analytics_pushdown_available` so the page can describe the mode up-front.
   `/metrics/latest` needed no change — `mode` rides along in `**result`.
9. src/shared/config/settings.py
   Add `analytics_pushdown_enabled` (default true) and `analytics_top_n`
   (default 100); reword `analytics_max_triples` as the mode switch point.
10. src/front/templates/partials/dtwin/_query_analytics.html
    The over-limit banner informs instead of blocking when pushdown is
    available, and Run Analysis stays enabled. Charts for unavailable metrics
    explain why rather than drawing a flat zero line. Fix the completion
    notification, which derived the node count from `Object.keys(nodes).length`
    — wrong now that `nodes` is a bounded slice. Correct the "filters narrow the
    charts, not the load" copy.
11. tests/units/core/test_pushdown_metrics.py
    New: 35 tests. A `SqliteStore` subclass of `GraphDBBackend` executes the
    generated SQL for real (SQLite needs only `least` / `greatest` registered),
    so the queries are validated rather than string-matched. `TestPushdownParity`
    asserts the SQL and NetworkX paths agree on structure stats, degree
    centrality and every type-profile field; `TestAnalysisTriplePushdown` checks
    the pushed-down triple selection against its Python oracle.
12. tests/units/core/test_graph_metrics.py
    Replace the bare `MagicMock` store with a `_FakeStore` implementing the
    pushdown contract via the backend's own filter, so filtered tests exercise
    real selection semantics instead of a mock returning whatever it is asked.
13. docs/user-guide.md, docs/get-started.md
    Document the two compute modes, what engine-side aggregation does and does
    not report, filter pushdown as the route to the full metric set on a large
    graph, and the two new environment variables.

